WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.191 --> 00:00:09.458
<v Daniel>You know, when we picture a scientific breakthrough, we usually imagine like a solitary genius staring at a chalkboard, suddenly connecting all the dots.</v>

2
00:00:09.778 --> 00:00:11.940
<v Maya>Right. Yeah. The classic Einstein image.</v>

3
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:27.192
<v Daniel>Exactly. But lately, with the whole explosion of AI, that picture is completely changed. Now the hype tells us to picture a black box. You feed in a prompt and boom, out pops a fully formed paradigm shifting paper.</v>

4
00:00:27.822 --> 00:00:42.735
<v Maya>Oh, the famous autonomous AI scientist myth. It's, you know, it's this pervasive idea in the tech world right now that we can just hand the steering wheel over to a language model, go to sleep and wake up to find it has independently cured a major disease or, I don't</v>

5
00:00:42.755 --> 00:00:44.477
<v Maya>know, finally solved quantum gravity.</v>

6
00:00:44.797 --> 00:00:57.956
<v Daniel>Right. And it sounds amazing, right? But today, we are looking at a brand new, just fascinating piece of work released literally today, October 1st, 2026, that completely shatters that myth.</v>

7
00:00:58.457 --> 00:00:59.518
<v Maya>Yeah, it really does.</v>

8
00:00:59.978 --> 00:01:13.530
<v Daniel>We're taking a deep dive into a guest post by Harvard physicist Matthew Schwartz, and it's titled Clawed-Shaped Science. He released this alongside a really detailed technical paper introducing an open source toolkit called Boot Loops.</v>

9
00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:27.262
<v Maya>And it's a provocative drop, honestly, because Schwartz is arguing something deeply counterintuitive for a tech-heavy paper. He says that while these massive language models are phenomenally good at doing science, they are absolutely not scientists.</v>

10
00:01:27.643 --> 00:01:38.633
<v Daniel>Which is a huge distinction. And that brings us to our mission today. We are going to cut through the noise, cut through the Silicon Valley hype, to figure out what AI is actually doing in the lab right now.</v>

11
00:01:39.232 --> 00:01:45.816
<v Maya>Yeah, exactly. To understand what AI can do, we first have to understand the reality of what it currently struggles to do.</v>

12
00:01:46.056 --> 00:02:00.884
