<v Maya>If we connect this to the bigger picture, Schwartz's thesis is fundamentally about workflow. AI does not collapse the scientific method into a single magical prompt. Rather, AI sits inside the scientific loop.</v>

141
00:20:21.476 --> 00:20:21.696
<v Daniel>Right.</v>

142
00:20:21.956 --> 00:20:33.082
<v Maya>It augments the incredibly tedious process of acquiring data, running the complex math, checking the code, and stepping forward. It is a spectacular power tool, not a replacement for the craftsman.</v>

143
00:20:33.690 --> 00:20:45.179
<v Daniel>So to quickly recap our journey today, we started with the agonizing hand-holding of vibe physics just six months ago, where Claude needed 110 drafts to do one calculation.</v>

144
00:20:45.520 --> 00:20:46.000
<v Maya>A nightmare.</v>

145
00:20:46.360 --> 00:21:01.314
<v Daniel>Truly. We explored how Schwartz solved that impedance mismatch by building boot loops, ensuring the model never supplies a digit, and targeting those Claude-shaped problems in the convex hole between disciplines. We saw how it magically linked quantum</v>

146
00:21:01.354 --> 00:21:03.597
<v Daniel>particle physics to primate family trees.</v>

147
00:21:04.458 --> 00:21:04.658
<v Maya>Yeah.</v>

148
00:21:05.139 --> 00:21:15.894
<v Daniel>And we saw, through the forest ecology example, that while AI brings unmatched computational breadth, human beings are desperately needed to supply the taste, the direction, and the actual meaning.</v>

149
00:21:16.398 --> 00:21:31.143
<v Maya>And that reality leaves us with some profound, unresolved questions for the future of academia. Schwartz points out that in this new paradigm, he honestly doesn't know how to train graduate students anymore. If bootloops can do all the coding and the grueling math,</v>

150
00:21:31.543 --> 00:21:35.624
<v Maya>what exactly is the entry-level work that teaches a Yang student how to be a scientist?</v>

151
00:21:35.784 --> 00:21:43.647
<v Daniel>Right. If the traditional hazing ritual of doing 100 integrals by hand is gone, how do they ever develop that human taste?</v>

152
00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:59.560
<v Maya>Furthermore, who actually gets the credit for this clod-shaped science? We risk a situation where the senior researcher gets all the glory and the hands-on prompting, curating, and AI-wrangling work is dismissed as just typing. And practically speaking, why would a</v>

153
00:21:59.620 --> 00:22:06.244
<v Maya>researcher write a grueling three-year grant proposal for a problem that an AI model with bootloops might solve over a single weekend?</v>

154
00:22:07.222 --> 00:22:22.061
<v Daniel>The pace of the technology is just completely outstripping our human institutions. It's thrilling, but it's incredibly disruptive. And that leaves us with one final thought for you to mull over today. If the real, untapped power of AI</v>

155
00:22:22.141 --> 00:22:36.824
<v Daniel>lies in the convex whole of human knowledge, its unique ability to connect completely isolated islands of expertise, what about your own life? What two completely unrelated fields in your own work, your hobbies, or your</v>

156
00:22:36.864 --> 00:22:46.899
<v Daniel>background might secretly hold the exact answers to each other's problems? Just waiting for you to find the right tool to connect them. Until next time, keep diving deep.</v>
